Chapter: DeskWindowRegistryTest
  cites Works chapter GopDraw
  cites Works chapter GopDesk
  cites Works chapter GopFont
  cites Foreword chapter Console

 Which windows are open and in what order they stack. The registry is pure
 arithmetic over one block, so alt-tab, click-to-focus, opening, closing and
 the stacking order are all decidable here without a screen, a face or a
 volume -- which is the reason this arm exists as well as the pictures: a
 focus cycle verified only by looking at a capture is verified by the slowest
 instrument available, and only for the two windows that happened to be open.

 EVERY ROW PRINTS THE WHOLE ARRAY, not just the count and the top. The array
 order IS the z order, so a row that printed only the top would agree with an
 implementation that dropped every window beneath it, and a row that printed
 only the count would agree with one that stacked them backwards. The payload
 is the assertion; the count and the top are conveniences beside it.

 The ids are `desk-focus-clkp` (5) and `desk-focus-calc` (4), printed as
 numbers rather than names because the number is what the block holds and a
 name would be this file's opinion about it.

Section: Body

  wr-item : Integer, Integer, Integer -> Text
  wr-item (ws) (i) (n) =
    if i >= n then ""
    else " " & integer-to-text (dk-wr-at ws i) & wr-item ws (i + 1) n

  wr-show : Text, Integer -> Text
  wr-show (label) (ws) =
    label & ": [" & wr-item ws 0 (dk-wr-count ws) & " ]"
    & "  count " & integer-to-text (dk-wr-count ws)
    & "  top " & integer-to-text (dk-wr-top ws)

 THE CAP, which nothing else reaches. `dk-wr-max` is the registry BLOCK's own
 bound -- 64 bytes, four for the count and four an entry, so fifteen fit and a
 sixteenth writes past it -- and there are fewer windowed panes than that, so
 the only way this is ever exercised is here. A registry that wrote past its
 block would corrupt whatever the arena put after it and read as working for
 exactly as long as nobody opened enough windows.

 It is a REFUSAL and not an eviction: the sixteenth window does not open and
 the fifteen already open are untouched. Evicting the bottom window instead
 would leave a pane whose step is still reachable through `desk-focus-cell`
 with no entry in the registry, which is a window that cannot be closed.

 The ids are the focus ids 1 to 16, used as bare numbers because the registry
 stores numbers and reads them back without ever asking what pane one names.
 That is the honest way to reach a cap higher than the number of panes that
 have windows.

  wr-fill : Integer, Integer, Integer -> Integer
  wr-fill (ws) (i) (n) =
    if i > n then 0
    else let a = dk-wr-open ws i
    in wr-fill ws (i + 1) n

  wr-overflow : Integer -> Text
  wr-overflow (ws) =
    let a = wr-fill ws 1 16
    in "sixteen into fifteen: count " & integer-to-text (dk-wr-count ws)
       & "  top " & integer-to-text (dk-wr-top ws)
       & "  cap " & integer-to-text dk-wr-max
       & "  bottom " & integer-to-text (dk-wr-at ws 0)
       & "  the sixteenth is absent " & wf-yn2 (dk-wr-find ws 16 0 < 0)

 EVERY CASCADED WINDOW STAYS INSIDE THE CONTENT BOX, and the positions stay
 distinct past the wrap. Those are two questions and a single span answers the
 first while failing the second: wrapping both axes together puts the window
 after the wrap exactly under the first one, which is the collision the
 cascade exists to prevent and which nothing but a photograph has ever caught.
 So this asks for the worst offset over the cap and for the number of DISTINCT
 (x, y) pairs the cascade produces.

  wr-pairs : Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer -> Integer
  wr-pairs (w) (h) (ds) (i) (n) (acc) =
    if i >= n then acc
    else wr-pairs w h ds (i + 1) n (if wr-dup w h ds i 0 then acc else acc + 1)

  wr-dup : Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer -> Boolean
  wr-dup (w) (h) (ds) (i) (j) =
    if j >= i then False
    else if dk-wnd-cascade-x j w == dk-wnd-cascade-x i w & dk-wnd-cascade-y j w h ds == dk-wnd-cascade-y i w h ds then True
    else wr-dup w h ds i (j + 1)

  wr-worst-x : Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer -> Integer
  wr-worst-x (w) (i) (n) (acc) =
    if i >= n then acc
    else let ox = dk-wnd-cascade-x i w
    in wr-worst-x w (i + 1) n (if ox > acc then ox else acc)

  wr-worst-y : Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer -> Integer
  wr-worst-y (w) (h) (ds) (i) (n) (acc) =
    if i >= n then acc
    else let oy = dk-wnd-cascade-y i w h ds
    in wr-worst-y w h ds (i + 1) n (if oy > acc then oy else acc)

  wr-cascade-room : Integer, Integer, Integer -> Text
  wr-cascade-room (w) (h) (ds) =
    let mx = dk-cbox-w w / 8
    in let my = dk-cbox-h w h ds / 8
    in let bx = wr-worst-x w 0 dk-wr-max 0
    in let by = wr-worst-y w h ds 0 dk-wr-max 0
    in "cascade at " & integer-to-text w & "x" & integer-to-text h
       & ": step " & integer-to-text (dk-wnd-step-px w)
       & "  margins " & integer-to-text mx & " and " & integer-to-text my
       & "  worst " & integer-to-text bx & " and " & integer-to-text by
       & "  inside both " & wf-yn2 (bx <= mx & by <= my)
       & "  distinct of " & integer-to-text dk-wr-max & ": " & integer-to-text (wr-pairs w h ds 0 dk-wr-max 0)

 THE CASCADE, and it is here because a picture caught what every row above
 misses. The registry, the painter and the focus cycle were all correct with
 two windows open and the SCREEN still showed one, because the default rect is
 a function of the screen alone and both windows took it. Nothing in a count,
 a top or an array order can see that; the question is whether the two rects
 DIFFER, so that is what this asks.

 `desk-wnd-open` needs a `ds` with real pane blocks behind cells 24 and 60,
 because it reads each pane's block to place it. That is the same requirement
 the desk has and the reason `desk-run` allocates those blocks.

  wr-rect : Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer -> Text
  wr-rect (blk) (w) (h) (ds) =
    let st = dk-wnd-st blk
    in "x " & integer-to-text (dk-wnd-rx blk w st)
       & " y " & integer-to-text (dk-wnd-ry blk w h st ds)
       & " w " & integer-to-text (dk-wnd-rw blk w st)
       & " h " & integer-to-text (dk-wnd-rh blk w h st ds)

  wr-cascade : Integer, Integer, Integer -> Text
  wr-cascade (w) (h) (ds) =
    let clk = peek-32 ds desk-clock-cell
    in let cal = peek-32 ds 24
    in let a = desk-wnd-open desk-focus-clkp w h ds
    in let b = desk-wnd-open desk-focus-calc w h ds
    in "w " & integer-to-text w
       & "  first " & wr-rect clk w h ds
       & "  second " & wr-rect cal w h ds
       & "  they differ " & wf-yn2 (dk-wnd-rx clk w dk-wnd-st-normal /= dk-wnd-rx cal w dk-wnd-st-normal & dk-wnd-ry clk w h dk-wnd-st-normal ds /= dk-wnd-ry cal w h dk-wnd-st-normal ds)
       & "  step " & integer-to-text (dk-wnd-step-px w)

  wf-yn2 : Boolean -> Text
  wf-yn2 (v) = if v then "yes" else "no"

 MINIMISE AND RESTORE. A minimised window stays IN the registry and keeps its
 rect; what changes is that it is not drawn and that focus must skip it. Those
 are two different questions and the rows below ask both, because a
 `dk-wr-visible` that returned the registry top regardless would look correct
 in every count and stack the desk on a pane nobody can see.

 The discriminating pair is `top` against `visible`: they agree until a window
 is minimised and MUST disagree while the top one is, which is the whole of
 what focus-skips-minimised means. When every window is minimised, `visible`
 is `none` and the desk is what shows.

 The restore asks the question 6.3c's stored rect exists to answer: the window
 comes back to the rect it left, so this compares the rect before minimising
 against the rect after restoring rather than merely checking the state flag.

  wr-vis : Text, Integer, Integer -> Text
  wr-vis (label) (ws) (ds) =
    label & ": [" & wr-item ws 0 (dk-wr-count ws) & " ]"
    & "  top " & integer-to-text (dk-wr-top ws)
    & "  visible " & integer-to-text (dk-wr-visible ws ds)

  wr-min-open : Integer, Integer, Integer -> Text
  wr-min-open (w) (h) (ds) =
    let a = desk-wnd-open desk-focus-clkp w h ds
    in let b = desk-wnd-open desk-focus-calc w h ds
    in wr-vis "both open     " (peek-32 ds dk-wr-cell) ds

  wr-min-one : Integer, Integer -> Text
  wr-min-one (ds) (fid) =
    let m = poke-32 (desk-wnd-blk fid ds) dk-wnd-st-cell dk-wnd-st-min
    in wr-vis "minimised one " (peek-32 ds dk-wr-cell) ds

  wr-min-both : Integer, Integer -> Text
  wr-min-both (ds) (fid) =
    let m = poke-32 (desk-wnd-blk fid ds) dk-wnd-st-cell dk-wnd-st-min
    in wr-vis "minimised both" (peek-32 ds dk-wr-cell) ds

  wr-restore : Integer, Integer -> Text
  wr-restore (ds) (fid) =
    let r = poke-32 (desk-wnd-blk fid ds) dk-wnd-st-cell dk-wnd-st-normal
    in let t = dk-wr-raise (peek-32 ds dk-wr-cell) fid
    in wr-vis "restored calc " (peek-32 ds dk-wr-cell) ds

 A THIRD WINDOWED PANE. Joining the window world is three arms -- a block, a
 title and a tree -- and the block is the one that fails silently. A pane
 `desk-wnd-blk` has no arm for answers ZERO, which is a writable address on
 this platform, so its window places itself over physical page zero and reads
 the rect back from whatever else lives there. A pane given the block of a
 pane that already has one is the other half: two windows in one rectangle,
 which is exactly the failure the cascade exists to prevent and which no
 count, top or array order can express.

 So the row asks for the block by identity rather than for a rect that looks
 plausible, and it asks the three positions to differ pairwise rather than
 asking whether the newest one moved.

 Sabotaged by deleting the console arm from `desk-wnd-blk`, three fields move
 and the registry fields do not: `its own block` goes to no, `positions
 distinct` goes to no, and the rect reads `x 300 y 135`, which is the
 never-placed default because page zero reads back as an unwritten block. The
 array, the count and the top are identical in both arms, which is the whole
 reason this row exists beside them.

  wr-xy : Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer -> Integer
  wr-xy (blk) (w) (h) (ds) =
    dk-wnd-rx blk w dk-wnd-st-normal * 100000 + dk-wnd-ry blk w h dk-wnd-st-normal ds

  wr-third : Integer, Integer, Integer -> Text
  wr-third (w) (h) (ds) =
    let clk = peek-32 ds desk-clock-cell
    in let cal = peek-32 ds 24
    in let con = peek-32 ds desk-console-cell
    in let a = desk-wnd-open desk-focus-clkp w h ds
    in let b = desk-wnd-open desk-focus-calc w h ds
    in let c = desk-wnd-open desk-focus-con w h ds
    in wr-show "three open    " (peek-32 ds dk-wr-cell)
       & "  console title " & desk-wnd-title desk-focus-con
       & "  its own block " & wf-yn2 (con /= 0 & desk-wnd-blk desk-focus-con ds == con)
       & "  blocks distinct " & wf-yn2 (con /= clk & con /= cal & clk /= cal)
       & "  positions distinct " & wf-yn2 (wr-xy clk w h ds /= wr-xy cal w h ds & wr-xy cal w h ds /= wr-xy con w h ds & wr-xy clk w h ds /= wr-xy con w h ds)
       & "  console " & wr-rect con w h ds

 A PANE THAT WANTS THE WHOLE CONTENT BOX. The launcher's tree is sized for the
 region the chrome used to hand it, so three quarters of that box clips the
 tail of each column, and `desk-wnd-open` gives it the box instead. The row
 asks for EQUALITY with the content box rather than for a plausible-looking
 rect, because the failure it guards is an offset: a full-box window that
 cascaded like an ordinary one would be a box's worth of content pushed one
 step off the bottom, which reads as a window that merely sits low.

 It opens the clock FIRST so the cascade would fire if it were going to, and
 it asks the clock to be unmoved so a bug that gave every window the box would
 not pass here.

  wr-box : Integer, Integer, Integer -> Text
  wr-box (w) (h) (ds) =
    let clk = peek-32 ds desk-clock-cell
    in let prg = peek-32 ds desk-prog-cell
    in let a = desk-wnd-open desk-focus-clkp w h ds
    in let b = desk-wnd-open desk-focus-prog w h ds
    in "launcher: " & wr-rect prg w h ds
       & "  is the content box " & wf-yn2 (dk-wnd-rx prg w dk-wnd-st-normal == dk-cbox-x w & dk-wnd-ry prg w h dk-wnd-st-normal ds == dk-cbox-y w & dk-wnd-rw prg w dk-wnd-st-normal == dk-cbox-w w & dk-wnd-rh prg w h dk-wnd-st-normal ds == dk-cbox-h w h ds)
       & "  clock unmoved " & wf-yn2 (dk-wnd-rx clk w dk-wnd-st-normal == dk-wnd-x w dk-wnd-st-normal)
       & "  wants the box: launcher " & wf-yn2 (dk-wnd-wants-box desk-focus-prog) & " clock " & wf-yn2 (dk-wnd-wants-box desk-focus-clkp)

 THE TWO QUIET PANES, which are the first to have needed a block at all. Every
 pane windowed before this one kept state and the window slot at 44 through 60
 was free space its block already carried; the Calendar and the Diffusion pane
 keep nothing, so each takes a `ds` cell of its own pointing at a block of its
 own. Two panes sharing one block is the collision `desk-wnd-blk`'s own
 paragraph describes, and a cell nobody wrote reads zero, so the row asks for
 both blocks by identity and asks them to differ.

 Both want the whole content box, which was MEASURED and not predicted: at
 1600x900 a three-quarter window is 480 by 300 in the logical units a tree is
 laid out in, against 840 by 546 at 1280x800, and each pane lost its last line
 at the larger size. The row asks the placement for equality with the box.

  wr-quiet : Integer, Integer, Integer -> Text
  wr-quiet (w) (h) (ds) =
    let cal = peek-32 ds dk-cal-cell
    in let dif = peek-32 ds dk-dif-cell
    in let a = desk-wnd-open desk-focus-cal w h ds
    in let b = desk-wnd-open desk-focus-dif w h ds
    in "quiet panes: " & desk-wnd-title desk-focus-cal & " and " & desk-wnd-title desk-focus-dif
       & "  own blocks " & wf-yn2 (cal /= 0 & dif /= 0 & cal /= dif & desk-wnd-blk desk-focus-cal ds == cal & desk-wnd-blk desk-focus-dif ds == dif)
       & "  both want the box " & wf-yn2 (dk-wnd-wants-box desk-focus-cal & dk-wnd-wants-box desk-focus-dif)
       & "  both placed at it " & wf-yn2 (dk-wnd-rx cal w dk-wnd-st-normal == dk-cbox-x w & dk-wnd-rw cal w dk-wnd-st-normal == dk-cbox-w w & dk-wnd-rx dif w dk-wnd-st-normal == dk-cbox-x w & dk-wnd-rw dif w dk-wnd-st-normal == dk-cbox-w w)
       & "  registry [" & wr-item (peek-32 ds dk-wr-cell) 0 (dk-wr-count (peek-32 ds dk-wr-cell)) & " ]"

Section: Entry

 The block is bound ONCE with a `let`. A nullary definition is a recipe and
 not a cell, so every mention of one would hand back a fresh zeroed block and
 every row below would report an empty registry while reading as a pass.

 The sequence is one story told in order, because each row is the state the
 row above left behind: that is what makes a wrong ORDER visible rather than
 just a wrong answer.

  opening : [Console] Nothing =
    let ws = alloc-zeroed 64 64
    in act
      print-line-uni (wr-show "empty          " ws)
      let a = dk-wr-open ws desk-focus-clkp
      in act
        print-line-uni (wr-show "open clock     " ws)
        let b = dk-wr-open ws desk-focus-calc
        in act
          print-line-uni (wr-show "open calc      " ws)
          let c = dk-wr-cycle ws
          in act
            print-line-uni (wr-show "cycle          " ws)
            let d = dk-wr-cycle ws
            in act
              print-line-uni (wr-show "cycle back     " ws)
              let e = dk-wr-raise ws desk-focus-clkp
              in act
                print-line-uni (wr-show "raise clock    " ws)
                let f = dk-wr-open ws desk-focus-calc
                in act
                  print-line-uni (wr-show "reopen calc    " ws)
                  let g = dk-wr-close ws (dk-wr-top ws)
                  in act
                    print-line-uni (wr-show "close the top  " ws)
                    let i2 = dk-wr-close ws desk-focus-mon
                    in act
                      print-line-uni (wr-show "close a stranger" ws)
                      let j = dk-wr-cycle ws
                      in act
                        print-line-uni (wr-show "cycle over one " ws)
                        let k = dk-wr-close ws (dk-wr-top ws)
                        in act
                          print-line-uni (wr-show "close the last " ws)
                          let l = dk-wr-cycle ws
                          in act
                            print-line-uni (wr-show "cycle over none" ws)
                            print-line-uni ("ids: clock " & integer-to-text desk-focus-clkp & " calc " & integer-to-text desk-focus-calc & " none " & integer-to-text desk-focus-none & " max " & integer-to-text dk-wr-max)
                            print-line-uni (wr-overflow (alloc-zeroed 64 64))
                            let cds = alloc-zeroed 256 64
                            in let c1 = poke-32 cds dk-wr-cell (alloc-zeroed 64 64)
                            in let c2 = poke-32 cds desk-clock-cell (alloc-zeroed 64 64)
                            in let c3 = poke-32 cds 24 (alloc-zeroed 64 64)
                            in act
                              print-line-uni (wr-cascade 1280 800 cds)
                              let mds = alloc-zeroed 256 64
                              in let d1 = poke-32 mds dk-wr-cell (alloc-zeroed 64 64)
                              in let d2 = poke-32 mds desk-clock-cell (alloc-zeroed 64 64)
                              in let d3 = poke-32 mds 24 (alloc-zeroed 64 64)
                              in act
                                print-line-uni (wr-min-open 1280 800 mds)
                                print-line-uni ("the rect calc leaves: " & wr-rect (peek-32 mds 24) 1280 800 mds)
                                print-line-uni (wr-min-one mds desk-focus-calc)
                                print-line-uni (wr-min-both mds desk-focus-clkp)
                                print-line-uni (wr-restore mds desk-focus-calc)
                                print-line-uni ("the rect calc returns to: " & wr-rect (peek-32 mds 24) 1280 800 mds)
                                print-line-uni ("minimised now: calc " & wf-yn2 (dk-wnd-minimised (peek-32 mds 24)) & " clock " & wf-yn2 (dk-wnd-minimised (peek-32 mds desk-clock-cell)))
                                print-line-uni ("states: normal " & integer-to-text dk-wnd-st-normal & " max " & integer-to-text dk-wnd-st-max & " min " & integer-to-text dk-wnd-st-min)
                                let tds = alloc-zeroed 256 64
                                in let e1 = poke-32 tds dk-wr-cell (alloc-zeroed 64 64)
                                in let e2 = poke-32 tds desk-clock-cell (alloc-zeroed 64 64)
                                in let e3 = poke-32 tds 24 (alloc-zeroed 64 64)
                                in let e4 = poke-32 tds desk-console-cell (alloc-zeroed 64 64)
                                in act
                                  print-line-uni (wr-third 1280 800 tds)
                                  let bds = alloc-zeroed 256 64
                                  in let f1 = poke-32 bds dk-wr-cell (alloc-zeroed 64 64)
                                  in let f2 = poke-32 bds desk-clock-cell (alloc-zeroed 64 64)
                                  in let f3 = poke-32 bds desk-prog-cell (alloc-zeroed 64 64)
                                  in act
                                    print-line-uni (wr-box 1280 800 bds)
                                    print-line-uni (wr-cascade-room 1280 800 bds)
                                    print-line-uni (wr-cascade-room 1600 900 bds)
                                    let qds = alloc-zeroed 256 64
                                    in let g1 = poke-32 qds dk-wr-cell (alloc-zeroed 64 64)
                                    in let g2 = poke-32 qds dk-cal-cell (alloc-zeroed 64 64)
                                    in let g3 = poke-32 qds dk-dif-cell (alloc-zeroed 64 64)
                                    in print-line-uni (wr-quiet 1280 800 qds)
                                  end
                                end
                              end
                            end
                          end
                        end
                      end
                    end
                  end
                end
              end
            end
          end
        end
      end
    end

